WEBVTT

00:00:01.550 --> 00:00:09.546
<v Faheem>Sabir, that cow stopped grazing a while ago, but it’s still chewing. Where did the grass it’s chewing come from?

00:00:12.846 --> 00:00:17.255
<v Sabir>I have some arrows for a model. Let’s start with one mouthful of grass.

00:00:17.805 --> 00:00:24.755
<v Faheem>Here is a real cow. We can see its coat and legs, but we can’t see inside its body.

00:00:28.056 --> 00:00:34.545
<v Sabir>I’ll put the grass at the start. When the cow swallows, the arrow points from the mouth towards the stomach.

00:00:37.845 --> 00:00:47.199
<v Faheem>A cow has one stomach divided into four compartments. In the rumen, tiny microbes help break down the fibre in grass.

00:00:50.498 --> 00:00:58.267
<v Sabir>If all my arrows point down, the grass can’t get back to the mouth. Our model still can’t explain what we saw.

00:00:58.817 --> 00:01:06.577
<v Faheem>Choose one more arrow. If the cow is going to chew that same grass again, where must the arrow point?

00:01:11.127 --> 00:01:18.976
<v Sabir>Back to the mouth! The cow brings up a lump of food it already swallowed and chews it again.

00:01:19.526 --> 00:01:25.921
<v Faheem>This is called chewing the cud. Chewing breaks the grass into smaller pieces, and the cow swallows it again.

00:01:29.221 --> 00:01:35.710
<v Sabir>There are sheep grazing in the next field. Does our returning arrow work for sheep too?

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:43.213
<v Faheem>It does. Sheep chew the cud too. Here are real sheep together in a flock.

00:01:46.513 --> 00:01:56.922
<v Sabir>Now I can answer. A cow can keep chewing after grazing because it brings its food back up. Thank Allah for creating these animals.
